# reopen-pane.sh.schema
#
# Companion document for `reopen-pane.sh`, the `pane.exited` hook.
#
# ## The event
#
# herdr runs the hook with the event in `HERDR_PLUGIN_EVENT_JSON`. For a pane
# that exits, the measured shape is:
#
# ```json
# {"event":"pane_exited","data":{"type":"pane_exited","pane_id":"w2:p2","workspace_id":"w2"}}
# ```
#
# Only `data.workspace_id` is used. The pane id is deliberately ignored: the
# pane is gone, and the hook's job is to open a new one in the same workspace
# (or in the workspace it recreates), not to resurrect a specific pane id.
#
# ## Decision order
#
# | Condition | Action |
# |-----------|--------|
# | no `data.workspace_id` in the event | exit 0 — not a pane exit we act on |
# | no roster file | exit 0 — the host has not booted yet |
# | the workspace is not in the roster | exit 0 — not an agent of this host |
# | the agent's stop marker exists | exit 0 — a deliberate stop is never undone by a fallback path |
# | more than `AGENT_REOPEN_LIMIT` reopens inside `AGENT_REOPEN_WINDOW` seconds | sleep `AGENT_REOPEN_BACKOFF` first |
# | `plugin pane open` succeeds | exit 0 |
# | it fails naming a missing workspace | recreate the workspace with the agent's cwd and label, rewrite the roster line with the new workspace id, open the pane, then close the root shell pane the create came with |
# | it fails any other way | exit 0 after logging: the workspace exists, so this is not the hook's failure to repair |
#
# ## Why the workspace may be gone
#
# herdr closes a workspace that loses its last pane — the auto-close cascade is
# pane, then tab, then workspace. When the wrapper is killed outright, that is
# exactly the sequence: the pane exits, it was often the workspace's only pane
# (the boot closes the shell pane once the agent pane is open), and the
# workspace is gone before this hook starts. The event still names the old
# workspace id, so the id in the roster is rewritten to the recreated one;
# without that, `agent-loop.sh` would resolve nothing and refuse.
#
# ## Inputs
#
# `HERDR_PLUGIN_EVENT_JSON`, `HERDR_BIN_PATH`, `HERDR_PLUGIN_STATE_DIR`,
# `HERDR_PLUGIN_CONFIG_DIR` (injected by herdr); `AGENT_STATE_ROOT`,
# `AGENT_ROSTER_FILE`, `AGENT_PLUGIN_ID`, `AGENT_PANE_ENTRYPOINT`,
# `AGENT_REOPEN_LIMIT`, `AGENT_REOPEN_WINDOW`, `AGENT_REOPEN_BACKOFF`
# (deployment env, defaults in the script).
#
# ## State
#
# `<state root>/reopen.log` (what the hook did and why), `<state root>/<agent
# id>/reopens` (timestamps for the bound), and the roster rewrite itself.
#
# ## Failure behaviour
#
# Exits 0 in every case except two: a workspace that must be recreated while the
# roster has no workspace directory for the agent, and a recreate or reopen that
# fails. Both print one line prefixed `reopen-pane: ` and exit 78. A hook that
# fails is visible in `herdr plugin log`, which is where an operator looks when
# a pane does not come back.
#
# ## Validation
#
# ```
# sh -n reopen-pane.sh
# # inside a running host, with an agent pane open:
# kill -9 <the pane's process>                       # not the agent: the wrapper itself
# herdr pane list --workspace <workspace id>         # a new pane id, same workspace
# herdr plugin log agent-respawn                     # the hook's own output
# ```
